Taken in two parts, SIDE=frontend then SIDE=backend. The first attempt at the
whole pass in one run was stopped partway through by the OOM killer and left a
mutant in the source -- the failure recorded in README.md from the Docker builds
that stopped two earlier passes, reached again from a different direction:
twenty-three mutants, most of them starting a container, is enough churn on its
own. The originals were restored from the guard file and the tree verified clean
before anything below was read.

That aborted run is also what found the one gap in this item's tests. Two
mutants adding a field to `SelectableRun` -- the scenario type, and a finding
count -- SURVIVED it:

  the chooser carries the kind of hour                          SURVIVED 118 passed
  the chooser carries how much was found                        SURVIVED 118 passed

Every assertion in the suite was about the shape that is *served*, and
`SelectableHour.of` names its four fields explicitly, so neither mutant reached
the wire. They are harmless and the gap is real: the item's claim is that the
guarantee is STRUCTURAL -- a shape with no field for the answer cannot leak it --
and it was structural at one boundary and nowhere else, while the layer below is
exactly where somebody would add the field, in the query, to make the chooser
more useful. One further edit in `of` and it is on the analyst's screen.

Closed with two tests over `SelectableRun`'s own field set, mirroring the ones
over `SelectableHour`'s. 1 test change, 0 implementation changes. Both mutants
are killed below.

